  "plan": {
    "goal": "Recapitulate the physiological low wall shear stress of lymphatic endothelium in a perfused microfluidic channel.",
    "rationale": "Lymphatic vessels experience slow, intermittent flow with wall shear stress well below the general microvascular range. Literature places lymphatic endothelial shear around 0.01–0.1 Pa (0.1–1 dyn/cm²), versus 0.1–1 Pa for blood microvasculature. I target 0.05 Pa (mid-low lymphatic range) using a 400×100 µm channel at 2 µL/min (medium viscosity 1e-3 Pa·s). This yields Re ≈ 0.13 (laminar), a 20 Pa pressure drop (negligible for a syringe pump), and a 24 s residence time. Seeding at 1.5e4 cells/cm² (within the HUVEC/endothelial 1e4–2e4 range) over the 0.08 cm² channel gives ~1200 cells. Note: the registry resolves 'lymphatic endothelial' to HUVEC (shear 0.1–1.0 Pa, the blood-microvascular convention); the lower lymphatic target used here is a deliberate physiological override and should be confirmed against primary lymphatic endothelial cell (LEC) literature.",
